      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n`ERC-20` mandates that the `Transfer` event always identify the token holder whose balance decreases in the `from` field\n\n```solidity\nevent Transfer(address indexed from, address indexed to, uint256 value);\n```\n\nBut in `TokenWrapper.sol` the `transferFrom` implementation emits the spender (`msg.sender`) as `from`, rather than the approved token owner (`from` parameter).  \n\n\n```solidity\nfunction transferFrom(address from, address to, uint256 amount) external returns (bool) {\n    ...\n    if (to == address(CORE)) {\n        coreBalance += amount;\n    } else {\n        _balanceOf[to] += amount;\n    }\n    emit Transfer(msg.sender, to, amount); // ❌ violates ERC-20\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\n## Impact\nERC-20 non-compliance – automated tooling may treat the token as invalid or malfunction when reconstructing balances from events.  \nIncorrect attribution – wallets, indexers, subgraphs, analytics and airdrop scripts will record the spender as the debited account, corrupting historical data, balances, and accounting.  \nOperational risk – once deployed, the incorrect events are immutable; integrators must add special-case logic or accept corrupted data.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nReplace the event emission with the correct parameters and add a regression test:\n\n```diff\n- emit Transfer(msg.sender, to, amount);\n+ emit Transfer(from,       to, amount);\n```",
